#
# system_ext_public/xrom_ota_installer.te — the only domain that may write the vault.
#
# This domain exists to hold exactly one permission: blk_file write on
# xrom_vault_device. Nothing else in the system has it, and the private policy carries
# a neverallow that says so in a form the build enforces rather than a comment that
# asks for it.
#
# WHY "READ-ONLY WHEN THE PROCESS EXITS" IS NOT A SELINUX FEATURE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The requirement this implements was phrased as "the vault becomes read-only when the
# OTA installer process exits". SELinux has no such mechanism: policy is static, a
# domain either holds a permission or it does not, and nothing about it changes with a
# process lifetime. There is no conditional permission and no time-based rule.
#
# The equivalent that does work, and that this policy implements:
#   1. the vault image partition is mounted ro and is never mounted rw — it is written
#      through the block device, which is how update_engine writes an inactive slot;
#   2. exactly one domain holds write, so "read-only" is true of every other domain at
#      every moment, not only after an exit;
#   3. the vault is covered by its own AVB hashtree descriptor, so a write that did not
#      come with a matching signed vbmeta is detected at the next boot rather than
#      trusted.
# Together those are strictly stronger than the phrased requirement: the vault is not
# read-only after the installer exits, it is read-only always and writable only through
# one auditable path.
#
# See correction #5 in docs/05.
#

type xrom_ota_installer, domain;
# /system_ext/bin/xrom_ota_installer.
type xrom_ota_installer_exec, exec_type, file_type, system_file_type;
